1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:21,338
So we're just looking at this virtue, parami, this perfection.

2
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:22,440
We'll come to that in a minute.

3
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:27,466
This word, the resolution, determination, adhitana.

4
00:00:27,486 --> 00:00:29,329
So we've got lots of words.

5
00:00:29,882 --> 00:00:49,238
persistence, tenacity, perseverance, doggedness, resolve, resolution, steadfast, willpower, purpose, purposeful, grit, and all these are life's little nuances to our understanding of determination.

6
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:56,304
So the word itself, Adi, it'd be split into Adi and Tana.

7
00:00:56,445 --> 00:00:59,787
Adi just means higher, above, superior, etc.,

8
00:01:00,156 --> 00:01:06,243
So you get it in words like adhichitta, superior mind, or adhipati, who's a ruler.

9
00:01:07,506 --> 00:01:18,379
Tāna is used both in a realistic sense, a place, a location, but metaphorically, it's a state, a condition, a reason, occasion.

10
00:01:20,141 --> 00:01:25,927
And most of you will know the Satipatthāna Sutta,

11
00:01:26,362 --> 00:01:30,489
So the Tana at the end is the establishment, the foundation.

12
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:37,161
The path is just reinforcing that, a deep and profound establishment of sati, awareness.

13
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:41,287
And that's the discourse on how to establish awareness.

14
00:01:43,271 --> 00:01:48,760
I like the word perseverance for some reason, you know, just dogged perseverance.

15
00:01:50,561 --> 00:01:59,353
In the Visuddhimagga, it talks about the resolution to undertake and carry out the requisites of awakening without wavering.

16
00:01:59,954 --> 00:02:12,772
It's an unwavering commitment, unshakable resolve.

17
00:02:14,068 --> 00:02:22,858
It also talks about an unshakable resolve directed towards a wholesome goal, grounded in wisdom and supported by the other perfections.

18
00:02:23,359 --> 00:02:35,211
So what we'll do is go through these perfections and show how Adhitana sort of is a binding force that brings them all together in order for us to attain a particular goal.

19
00:02:35,913 --> 00:02:41,899
Of course, here we're centred on liberation, but these things also work for even...

20
00:02:42,098 --> 00:02:58,998
you know, making a relationship work, some task at work itself, anything that you undertake, which is going to take effort, continued effort, all these factors come into play.

21
00:03:03,344 --> 00:03:11,514
So if we just go through the part of me, so generosity, you see, so when you set yourself a goal on something,

22
00:03:11,883 --> 00:03:15,007
It's being generous to yourself, isn't it?

23
00:03:15,526 --> 00:03:16,848
You're giving yourself something.

24
00:03:16,929 --> 00:03:18,150
You're giving yourself an aim.

25
00:03:19,050 --> 00:03:25,878
And because we are always interconnected, as you know, involving other people, it's also a gift for them.

26
00:03:28,161 --> 00:03:33,106
So that generosity comes in there somewhere, the feeling of giving.

27
00:03:33,167 --> 00:03:41,316
Now, when it comes to virtue, sila, ethical conduct, you can then see how

28
00:03:41,784 --> 00:03:44,328
If you don't have that, things can go a bit wrong.

29
00:03:44,948 --> 00:03:49,813
So somebody I think who has clear determination is Putin.

30
00:03:49,834 --> 00:03:51,556
I mean, he's prepared.

31
00:03:51,575 --> 00:03:59,243
When I say that, he's prepared to put so many millions, so many thousands of young men at the front to be killed and wounded.

32
00:04:00,324 --> 00:04:01,747
So it's not actually affecting him.

33
00:04:01,766 --> 00:04:04,149
He's probably enjoying caviar.

34
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:09,294
But at least he's got that determination to sacrifice everybody to that.

35
00:04:09,645 --> 00:04:13,913
final aim of expanding the Russian Federation.

36
00:04:14,955 --> 00:04:20,384
And of course, you'll see that in all of these authoritarians, these tyrants.

37
00:04:21,565 --> 00:04:27,214
And we can point to other people too, but we shan't go into that.

38
00:04:27,235 --> 00:04:34,927
So it has to be in terms of our practice of becoming fully liberated, then obviously it needs to be virtuous.

39
00:04:36,156 --> 00:04:41,223
Now, Nekama, renunciation, consider we've just had a little baby in the family.

40
00:04:42,165 --> 00:04:47,613
And when you think about, and many of you will have had children.

41
00:04:48,134 --> 00:04:53,262
So when you think about, you know, having a child, that's a huge commitment.

42
00:04:53,802 --> 00:04:55,524
And you have to have perseverance.

43
00:04:58,009 --> 00:05:05,860
I think it was Kabat-Zinn, some of you will know, you know, all that stuff about mindfulness training.

44
00:05:06,346 --> 00:05:09,810
He said bringing up children was like 18 years on retreat.

45
00:05:10,372 --> 00:05:15,057
So that sort of complete, you know, devotion.

46
00:05:15,077 --> 00:05:21,728
I mean, a baby is 24 hours, 24 hours, seven, you know, conditioning.

47
00:05:21,887 --> 00:05:25,192
And you have to you have to give up something.

48
00:05:25,452 --> 00:05:25,754
Yeah.

49
00:05:26,394 --> 00:05:29,338
You have to give up what you what you would normally have done.

50
00:05:29,579 --> 00:05:35,728
Go to, you know, go to the pictures or whatever, however you amuse yourselves.

51
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:39,973
So it involves some level of renunciation.

52
00:05:40,855 --> 00:05:42,718
Pana, so it's wisdom, of course.

53
00:05:44,579 --> 00:05:50,148
It's got to be a certain devotion to righteousness.

54
00:05:50,648 --> 00:06:01,041
I'm thinking here of people who really spend an enormous amount of energy trying to get injustices corrected.

55
00:06:02,483 --> 00:06:05,648
The one that comes to mind recently was the

56
00:06:06,589 --> 00:06:16,670
for those in the UK, was this huge miscarriage of justice about the post office where the powers that be trusted this accounting software.

57
00:06:17,432 --> 00:06:21,560
And it eventually ended up with 900 postmasters being dismissed.

58
00:06:22,281 --> 00:06:23,704
And there were suicides.

59
00:06:24,307 --> 00:06:29,334
And some went to jail, completely miscarried justice.

60
00:06:29,733 --> 00:06:39,745
And it was the actions of one man, in particular, Alan Bates, who spent 20 years getting justice.

61
00:06:39,805 --> 00:06:40,826
Can you imagine that?

62
00:06:41,447 --> 00:06:42,928
20 years perseverance.

63
00:06:43,829 --> 00:06:52,240
And that's the sort of wisdom, that idea for justice.

64
00:06:52,944 --> 00:06:56,149
Now, obviously, that's going to take energy, effort, right?

65
00:06:56,170 --> 00:07:00,435
Don't confuse effort with perseverance.

66
00:07:00,495 --> 00:07:08,105
Perseverance is a mental state, it's a mental attitude, rather, whereas effort is the energy you need, both the physical and mental energy.

67
00:07:10,689 --> 00:07:13,634
Not having any of these can undermine the whole project.

68
00:07:14,394 --> 00:07:21,264
And then, of course, there's patience, you know, like this man, 20 years, for heaven's sake, and to...

69
00:07:21,666 --> 00:07:26,074
you know, to actually have the patience and the willingness to endure.

70
00:07:26,494 --> 00:07:27,875
That's important too.

71
00:07:29,899 --> 00:07:33,805
And then we have satya, which is truthfulness.

72
00:07:33,925 --> 00:07:37,750
It's really being honest about oneself, about one's capabilities.

73
00:07:38,651 --> 00:07:45,682
Obviously, if you have an aim, which is, you know, beyond your capability, you just get burnout and disappointment.

74
00:07:46,504 --> 00:07:49,968
You know, I mean, if I started thinking about becoming a professional footballer,

75
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:52,935
And I got the kit and I went training.

76
00:07:54,959 --> 00:07:55,800
Turned up at clubs.

77
00:07:56,380 --> 00:08:05,774
It might be that a good friend of mine might come to tell me that maybe I was being a bit misguided, not enough humility.

78
00:08:08,178 --> 00:08:14,487
So there has to be that sense of being real about oneself.

79
00:08:15,649 --> 00:08:20,257
There's method, of course, doing anything which is wholesome.

80
00:08:20,499 --> 00:08:24,165
And here we're talking specifically about liberation.

81
00:08:24,745 --> 00:08:28,874
It's obviously an act of loving kindness, an act of care towards ourselves.

82
00:08:29,995 --> 00:08:35,245
And because it always involves other people, it therefore is an act of care towards others.

83
00:08:36,726 --> 00:08:41,171
And then finally, this quality of upeka, which is equanimity.

84
00:08:41,672 --> 00:09:03,173
So, you know, that's being able to bear with things, not to get caught up in disappointment, not to go berserk when something wonderful has happened, to keep that even keel, which of course reserves energy and helps us to be more narrowed in our focus when we want something.

85
00:09:06,460 --> 00:09:18,533
So you can see how once you've brought a certain sense of persistence, a certain sense of commitment to something, all your virtues come to support you.

86
00:09:22,538 --> 00:09:23,239
Gratitude.

87
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:31,388
Once you think about the things that have been given us, then the natural thing is to want to give back.

88
00:09:31,957 --> 00:09:36,342
So virtues always support each other in some way or other.

89
00:09:36,543 --> 00:09:36,764
Yeah.

90
00:09:37,183 --> 00:09:42,551
Whereas vices, anger, greed, they cancel each other out.

91
00:09:42,791 --> 00:09:48,258
You can't be angry and greedy at the same time, even though it might feel like that.

92
00:09:50,019 --> 00:09:52,582
There are two mental states that cancel each other out.

93
00:09:54,722 --> 00:10:04,375
So how much should we, you know, what should our determination be when it comes to this process of liberation?

94
00:10:04,836 --> 00:10:14,548
So this is taken from a particular discourse where the Buddha describes the sort of ascetic practices that he did.

95
00:10:14,628 --> 00:10:18,131
And he says, he starts off with this clear determination.

96
00:10:18,753 --> 00:10:25,221
Let only my skin, sinews and bones remain and let my flesh and blood dry up.

97
00:10:25,782 --> 00:10:32,191
I will not give up my effort so long as I have not attained what can be attained by human strength.

98
00:10:33,091 --> 00:10:44,827
Now, this took him into those ascetic practices like holding his breath for as long as he could, not eating, of course, almost starving himself to death.

99
00:10:45,414 --> 00:10:53,186
And at some point he realized that this was over-efforting, it was a wrong sort of perseverance.

100
00:10:53,225 --> 00:11:14,595
And with that, he leaves his companions, if you remember, and then he finds this lovely place, sorry, and then he remembers this occasion in his life, which according to the academics is the only bit of biography you can actually believe, where he remembers watching his father

101
00:11:14,827 --> 00:11:16,711
doing an opening plowing ceremony.

102
00:11:17,572 --> 00:11:27,366
And he's a young kid, and you know how young kids get involved, and he's totally involved in what his father's doing, drawn by curiosity, by interest.

103
00:11:28,469 --> 00:11:38,604
And this not only changes his practice from trying to attain something, you know, liberation, trying to attain us, to investigating his condition.

104
00:11:39,424 --> 00:11:43,991
And he realizes that when he was in that state, he was actually

105
00:11:44,258 --> 00:11:45,019
very happy.

106
00:11:45,919 --> 00:11:59,958
And now we call these things, when I say now, it's been only fairly, fairly recent in the past five, maybe seven, ten years, that the whole thing about sutta jhanas has come to the fore.

107
00:11:59,979 --> 00:12:11,734
So the old jhana was normally, they call them the visuddhi-maga jhana, where you become completely absorbed and lose all touch with the surroundings.

108
00:12:12,085 --> 00:12:17,269
I mean, they're beautiful, they're very enjoyable, but there's no insight in it.

109
00:12:17,910 --> 00:12:29,042
Whereas with these sutta jhanas, they're much lighter, you can still be aware of things, but it has that quality of curiosity, and so is a path to liberation.

110
00:12:30,001 --> 00:12:36,908
And this is what he begins to practice, and this is what he refers to when he talks about people doing jhana practice.

111
00:12:37,990 --> 00:12:40,793
It's a supportive thing to your enquiry.

112
00:12:46,712 --> 00:12:56,942
So, you know, it was a case of him realizing this when all the other stuff that the ascetics were practicing had failed him.

113
00:13:08,784 --> 00:13:12,789
There's a distinction to be made between that and willpower.

114
00:13:13,350 --> 00:13:22,380
So willpower in that sense of total commitment, but it's a sort of narrow vision.

115
00:13:22,980 --> 00:13:28,447
And you're not aware of the mountains and the chasms that are in your way.

116
00:13:29,048 --> 00:13:31,831
And you keep collapsing and push and push and push.

117
00:13:32,152 --> 00:13:34,153
And of course, it upsets everybody around you.

118
00:13:34,634 --> 00:13:36,256
It's willpower business, right?

119
00:13:36,397 --> 00:13:36,496
Yeah.

120
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:53,582
So you know from the reaction of people around you that you might be doing something which is ego-based, you know, pride-based, rather than coming from a sense of humility and of reality, you know, like being real about oneself and real about the situation.

121
00:13:54,563 --> 00:14:00,392
That often happens when we're practicing and we move into over-efforting.

122
00:14:00,845 --> 00:14:09,895
So that's the point where the determination, the perseverance has actually gone a bit wonky, right?

123
00:14:09,916 --> 00:14:15,162
Normally in meditation, if you're sitting and you do that, the meditation just collapses.

124
00:14:15,182 --> 00:14:16,524
You just can't do it for a while.

125
00:14:18,865 --> 00:14:27,797
So when Adhitana, this perseverance, this resolution is achieved,

126
00:14:28,131 --> 00:14:57,327
is developed there's always that clear understanding about what the goal is there's a moral integrity about it there's a consistent a consistency about your practice and about what you're devoted to and there's the ability to endure the ability to endure so and the Buddha sort of reinforces this

127
00:14:57,510 --> 00:15:12,077
When a bhikkhu, that means anybody who's listening, has resolution, has developed it and has made it his basis, now that's the ground of his practice, you might say, then he attains the destruction of the taints.

128
00:15:12,097 --> 00:15:19,991
These taints, the āsavā, they are sort of deep-seated mental defilements.

129
00:15:20,477 --> 00:15:33,692
They include, well, there's four of them, just the craving, craving, the craving for existence, you know, wanting to live, which is understandable, but it's that sort of attachment to life.

130
00:15:34,972 --> 00:15:38,517
Ignorance, ignorance about one's true identity.

131
00:15:38,677 --> 00:15:40,259
That's the basic problem.

132
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:47,166
And sometimes wrong view is attached to those arseholes, those taints.

133
00:15:47,186 --> 00:15:48,847
They're also called cankers.

134
00:15:49,115 --> 00:15:49,755
It's a bit odd.

135
00:15:50,096 --> 00:15:53,922
A canker is some sort of white thing on your tongue, isn't it?

136
00:15:53,961 --> 00:15:54,543
That hurts.

137
00:15:55,423 --> 00:15:57,025
I don't know.

138
00:15:57,066 --> 00:15:58,948
Somebody's called them fermentations.

139
00:16:00,250 --> 00:16:03,073
Translators do have a good time, I think.

140
00:16:03,094 --> 00:16:06,678
So finally, how do we actually develop this?

141
00:16:07,821 --> 00:16:12,287
So it's the small resolutions that you make that you keep.

142
00:16:12,654 --> 00:16:16,260
that builds up the muscle of perseverance.

143
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:30,908
So all of us have experienced, you know, perhaps being on retreat or on a Zoom or something, and you've made a resolution that you're going to sit every morning for half an hour, 40 minutes, one hour, whatever it is.

144
00:16:31,528 --> 00:16:33,432
And it works for two or three days.

145
00:16:34,441 --> 00:16:43,936
And then it all shrinks, it becomes 25 minutes, and then 20 minutes, and then it just all disappears, you know, and you wonder what's happening.

146
00:16:44,557 --> 00:16:51,386
So this is a lack of that resolution, a lack of that persistence, right, that perseverance.

147
00:16:52,024 --> 00:17:18,031
so one re-establishes it right and to establish a habit you've got to sort of put some energy into it some commitment and you must do it religiously for three months and then it becomes ingrained right not not to say that we don't get lazy again and we lose it we have to start all over again but that's the that's the uh the purpose of res resolutions throughout the day

148
00:17:18,433 --> 00:17:29,727
Sometimes, you know, and I've made this mistake myself, you make a resolution to help somebody and you've done it without real reflection.

149
00:17:30,268 --> 00:17:37,375
So I remember once a friend of mine said he was moving into a new flat and he was going to decorate on the weekend.

150
00:17:38,116 --> 00:17:40,559
And I just blurted out, oh, I'll come and help you, you see.

151
00:17:40,961 --> 00:17:46,851
Well, come Saturday morning, the last thing I wanted to do was decorate his apartment.

152
00:17:46,891 --> 00:17:48,553
But I was good.

153
00:17:48,792 --> 00:17:49,513
I was very good.

154
00:17:49,914 --> 00:17:55,063
I determined to go and I went and helped him out for the morning anyway.

155
00:17:55,723 --> 00:18:00,349
So sometimes we have to be careful what we resolve.

156
00:18:00,871 --> 00:18:02,993
It's a bit like being careful what you wish for.

157
00:18:05,617 --> 00:18:07,019
And...

158
00:18:09,582 --> 00:18:18,329
And to sort of praise ourselves when we've actually, you know, completed something which has taken a little bit of endurance.

159
00:18:19,310 --> 00:18:26,436
Often, I don't think we congratulate ourselves, you know, we don't have that sort of self-love, that self-praise.

160
00:18:27,238 --> 00:18:28,278
There's nothing wrong with that.

161
00:18:28,680 --> 00:18:28,920
Yeah.

162
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:32,083
As long as it doesn't get into an ego and how wonderful I am.

163
00:18:32,482 --> 00:18:36,006
But when we complete a task, we should stop and just say, oh, that was good.

164
00:18:36,145 --> 00:18:37,146
You know, I did good.

165
00:18:38,969 --> 00:18:39,048
Yes.

166
00:18:39,451 --> 00:18:46,674
And in that vein, it's good to remember past times when we persevered with something and achieved something.

167
00:18:47,297 --> 00:18:48,521
And that builds up confidence.

168
00:18:48,582 --> 00:18:49,865
We can do, you see.

169
00:18:51,701 --> 00:19:05,147
And so to end this off, there's always these little phrases that Arahats seem to say when somebody seems to say when they become fully liberated.

170
00:19:05,588 --> 00:19:07,172
And one of them is a lovely little thing.

171
00:19:07,531 --> 00:19:11,840
It just says, what had to be done has been done.

172
00:19:12,602 --> 00:19:14,065
And you can imagine the relief.

173
00:19:14,787 --> 00:19:16,648
Being able to say that.

174
00:19:16,669 --> 00:19:16,909
Right.

175
00:19:17,229 --> 00:19:20,231
So we said we set ourselves a task.

176
00:19:20,732 --> 00:19:22,193
We commit ourselves to it.

177
00:19:22,255 --> 00:19:24,636
We resolve to complete the task.

178
00:19:24,656 --> 00:19:28,820
Say you always have to have a little, you know, a small print.

179
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:29,301
Yeah.

180
00:19:29,682 --> 00:19:36,749
Save in emergency or extraordinary circumstance and you complete it.

181
00:19:36,929 --> 00:19:41,554
And then you can say to yourself, what had to be done has been done.

182
00:19:42,022 --> 00:19:44,748
And you get that sense of deep satisfaction.

183
00:19:45,107 --> 00:19:46,590
Yeah.

184
00:19:46,611 --> 00:19:47,152
Very good.

185
00:19:47,311 --> 00:19:59,813
I can only hope my words have been of some assistance and that by your unshakable resolve, you will attain full liberation from suffering sooner rather than later.

